There is a left proper combinatorial model structure $\sSet^{\dag}_{\Joyal}$ on $\sSet^{\dag}$ with
\begin{align}
\mathrm{Cof}&=I^{\sSet}_{\dag}\text{-}\cof=\{\text{free cofibrations}\},\\
f\in W^{\sSet}_{\dag}&\Longleftrightarrow\frakC_{\dag}(f)\in W_{\dag},\\
\mathrm{Fib}&=(\mathrm{Cof}\cap W^{\sSet}_{\dag})^{\square}.
\end{align}
The trivial cofibrations also have a small generating family.
A map $p$ is a trivial fibration if and only if $\UdagSSet(p)$ is a trivial Kan fibration.
Moreover, the original dagger rigidification--nerve adjunction
\begin{align}
\frakC_{\dag}:\sSet^{\dag}_{\Joyal}
\ \rightleftarrows\ \sCat^{\dag}_{\Bergner}:N_{\dag}
\end{align}
is a Quillen equivalence.

Let $f:X\to Y$ be a dagger morphism between dagger quasi-categories.
Full faithfulness means that, for every ordered pair of vertices $x,y\in X_0$, the induced map on the specified mapping fibers
\begin{align}
\Map_X(x,y)\to\Map_Y(fx,fy)
\end{align}
is a weak homotopy equivalence.
The following conditions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f$ belongs to $W^{\sSet}_{\dag}$, equivalently it is a weak equivalence in $\sSet^{\dag}_{\Joyal}$;
\item $f$ is fully faithful and $\pi_0\calU(f):\pi_0\calU(X)\to\pi_0\calU(Y)$ is surjective;
\item $f$ is fully faithful and $\calU(f):\calU(X)\to\calU(Y)$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.
\end{enumerate}
All conditions use the same original unitary-core functor $\calU$.
The full-faithfulness condition includes the case $x=y$.

There is a model structure $\sSet^{\AInv}_{\DCH}$ whose cofibrations are the original normal monomorphisms, whose weak equivalences are the prescribed anti-Joyal mapping weak equivalences, and whose fibrations are the prescribed underlying Joyal candidate fibrations.
For this model and the dagger Joyal model of Theorem B, neither of the original tagged-quotient adjunctions
\begin{align}
q_{\rmtag,!}:\sSet^{\AInv}_{\DCH}
&\ \rightleftarrows\ \sSet^{\dag}_{\Joyal}:q_{\rmtag}^*,\\
q_{\rmtag}^*:\sSet^{\dag}_{\Joyal}
&\ \rightleftarrows\ \sSet^{\AInv}_{\DCH}:q_{\rmtag,*}
\end{align}
is a Quillen adjunction.
In addition, restriction along the original inclusion $q_{\rmtag}^*$ does not recover the dagger Joyal cofibrations:
\begin{align}
\neg\,\forall f\text{ in }\sSet^{\dag},\quad
\Cof_{\Joyal}(f)\ \Longleftrightarrow\ \Cof_{\DCH}(q_{\rmtag}^*f).
\end{align}

Let $\prism_{\rev}$ be the reversal simplex category of Part~I.
For every universe $u$ there is an equivalence of categories
\begin{align}
  \sSet^{\dag}
    &\simeq \Fun(\prism_{\rev}^{\myop},\Set_u).
\end{align}
The dagger fixes vertices, and the actions of monotone and antitone maps in $\prism_{\rev}$ agree on their overlap.

For every universe $u$, the forgetful functor $\UdagSSet$ to ordinary simplicial sets has a left adjoint $\FdagSSet$:
\begin{align}
  \FdagSSet:\sSet&\to\sSet^{\dag},
  &\FdagSSet&\dashv\UdagSSet.
\end{align}

Let $\FdagSSet$ be any chosen left adjoint to $\UdagSSet$, with unit $\eta$.
For each simplicial set $A$ there is a natural second inclusion $r_A:A^{\myop}\to \UdagSSet\FdagSSet(A)$.
Writing $A^{(0)}=\sk_0A$ for the zeroth skeleton, the square
\begin{align}
  \begin{array}{ccc}
    A^{(0)}&\to&A^{\myop}\\
    \big\downarrow&&\big\downarrow\scriptstyle r_A\\
    A&\xrightarrow{\ \eta_A\ }&\UdagSSet\FdagSSet(A)
  \end{array}
\end{align}
is a pushout.
Moreover, $A^{(0)}$ is isomorphic to Lean's $(A.\mathrm{skeleton}\ 1).\mathrm{toSSet}$, and $\eta_A$ and $r_A$ satisfy both interchange equations for the dagger on the free object.
Lean's \texttt{skeleton 1} denotes the zeroth skeleton in this formalization, not the ordinary one-dimensional skeleton.

For every universe $u$ there is a small category $\mathcal I$ and an equivalence
\begin{align}
  \sGph^{\dag}
    &\simeq \Fun(\mathcal I^{\myop},\Set_u).
\end{align}
Consequently, dagger simplicial graphs are locally finitely presentable at level $u$ and admit all $u$-small limits and colimits.

In a common universe $u$, ordinary rigidification $\frakC:\sSet\to\sCat$, constructed as the left Kan extension of the standard simplex thickening, is left adjoint to the coherent nerve $N$.
The latter is naturally isomorphic to Mathlib's simplicial nerve, and rigidification of the standard simplices is naturally isomorphic to their thickening.
There is also a natural isomorphism
\begin{align}
  \frakC\circ\mathrm{op}_{\sSet}
    &\cong \mathrm{op}_{\sCat}\circ\frakC
\end{align}
compatible with the double-opposite comparison and the double-reversal map of simplicial sets.

In a common universe $u$, ordinary rigidification lifts to a functor
\begin{align}
  \frakC_{\dag}:\sSet^{\dag}
    &\to \sCat^{\dag}
\end{align}
with exactly the same underlying enriched functor.
It is left adjoint to the dagger coherent nerve $N_{\dag}$.
After forgetting the dagger, the unit and counit of $\frakC_{\dag}\dashv N_{\dag}$ agree with those of the original ordinary rigidification--coherent-nerve adjunction.

Let $C$ have object universe $w$ and Hom universe $v$, and suppose that $C$ has pullbacks.
For regular cardinals $\kappa_0,\kappa$ in universe $u$ with $\kappa_0\leq\kappa$,
\begin{align}
C\text{ is }\kappa_0\text{-locally presentable}
  \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
C\text{ is }\kappa\text{-locally presentable}.
\end{align}

Let $C$ be a model category in which every object is cofibrant, and let $L:C\to H$ be a localization of $C$ at its weak equivalences.
For every morphism $f$,
\begin{align}
f\text{ is a weak equivalence}
  \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
L(f)\text{ is an isomorphism in }H.
\end{align}
The object and Hom universes of $C$ and $H$ may be chosen independently.

For every value universe $u$, the categories of ordinary simplicial sets and bisimplicial sets are both locally finitely presentable relative to $u$:
\begin{align}
\sSet\text{ and }\operatorname{BiSSet}_u
  \quad\text{are locally finitely presentable}.
\end{align}

For anti-involutive simplicial sets, let $I_{\mathrm{nor}}$ be the family obtained by applying the original Part I free functor $F_{\AInv}$ to boundary inclusions in every degree.
The original class of normal monomorphisms is exactly the closure of $I_{\mathrm{nor}}$ under coproducts, pushouts, transfinite compositions, and retracts.
Indeed, every normal monomorphism admits a countable relative-cell presentation:
\begin{align}
\operatorname{NormalMono}
  = I_{\mathrm{nor}}\text{-}\cof.
\end{align}

For ordinary $\sSet$, let $W_{\Joyal}$ be the class of weak categorical equivalences tested by the homotopy categories of internal Homs into every quasi-category $Z$.
The specified Smith data satisfy their hypotheses and give a model structure with classes
\begin{align}
\Cof_{\Joyal} &= \operatorname{Mono}(\sSet), \\
W_{\Joyal} &= \{f\mid \forall Z\text{ a quasi-category},\;
       \h(Z^f)\text{ is an equivalence of categories}\}, \\
\Fib_{\Joyal} &= (\operatorname{Mono}(\sSet)\cap W_{\Joyal})^{\square}.
\end{align}
Here $Z^f$ denotes precomposition with $f$ on the internal Hom.
The identifications of the generated left and right classes are equalities for these specified Smith data.

In the category $\sSet^{\AInv}$ of anti-involutive simplicial sets, take cofibrations to be the original normal monomorphisms and weak equivalences to be the inverse image of $W_{\Joyal}$ under the forgetful functor.
Concrete Smith data and a second functorial factorization give a model structure $\sSet^{\AInv}_{\DCH}$.
For the forgetful functor $U_{\AInv}:\sSet^{\AInv}\to\sSet$, its three classes satisfy precisely
\begin{align}
\Cof_{\DCH} &= \operatorname{NormalMono}, \\
f\in W_{\DCH}
  &\Longleftrightarrow U_{\AInv}(f)\in W_{\Joyal}, \\
f\in\Fib_{\DCH}
  &\Longleftrightarrow U_{\AInv}(f)\in\Fib_{\Joyal}.
\end{align}
These are the classes of the same concrete model used in Theorem D; its two non-Quillen conclusions are not counted again here.
Nor does this construction assert a general right-induced transfer theorem for arbitrary right adjoints.
